# litpipe: config-driven literature ingestion & extraction

One codebase, any topic. Search terms, databases, filters and the extraction schema all live in a YAML file.

```
fetch (OpenAlex / PubMed / arXiv / Semantic Scholar) -> dedupe (DOI, then title) -> SQLite
  -> pdf (download OA PDFs, parse text with PyMuPDF, cache) -> extract (LLM -> JSON) -> export (CSV / MD / XLSX)
```

## 1. Install
```bash
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate      # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## 2. API keys (never put them in the YAML or in code)
Pick one:

**A. `.env` file (easiest).** `cp .env.example .env`, fill it in. `.env` is in `.gitignore`.

**B. Shell environment.**
```bash
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-..."          # macOS / Linux (add to ~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc to persist)
export CONTACT_EMAIL="you@university.edu"
```
```powershell
setx ANTHROPIC_API_KEY "sk-ant-..."            # Windows (open a new terminal afterwards)
setx CONTACT_EMAIL "you@university.edu"
```

| Variable | Needed for |
|---|---|
| `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` | `provider: anthropic` |
| `OPENAI_API_KEY` | `provider: openai` (not needed for local servers via `base_url`) |
| `CONTACT_EMAIL` | strongly recommended: OpenAlex/NCBI polite use; **required for Unpaywall PDF lookups** |
| `NCBI_API_KEY`, `SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_API_KEY`, `OPENALEX_API_KEY` | optional, higher rate limits |

## 3. Run
```bash
cp config.example.yaml config_project.yaml     # edit queries + extraction fields
python main.py --config config_project.yaml --dry-run          # check queries and see the exact LLM prompt
python main.py --config config_project.yaml --lookback-days 3650 --limit 5   # small first test
python main.py --config config_project.yaml                    # everything, using the config's lookback_days
```
Useful flags: `--stages fetch,pdf` (any subset of `fetch,pdf,extract,export`), `--force-extract`, `--retry-failed`, `--limit N`, `-v`.

Outputs in `output_dir`: `papers.db` (all data), `pdfs/`, `cache/text/`, `exports/master_summary.{csv,md,xlsx}`, `exports/papers_metadata.csv`, `pipeline.log`.

## 4. New topic
Copy the config, change `project`, `search.queries` and `extraction.fields`, point `output_dir` somewhere new. Nothing else.

## 5. Tuning prompts without re-downloading
PDFs are cached by DOI/ID, parsed text by file hash. Edit `extraction.fields` / `instructions`, then
`python main.py --config config_project.yaml --stages extract,export`. Changing fields, instructions or model creates a new
"schema hash", so only extraction re-runs and old results are kept in the DB. Use `--limit 5` while iterating.

## 6. Weekly schedule
Set `lookback_days: 7`; duplicates from overlapping windows are merged automatically.
```cron
0 7 * * MON  cd /path/to/litpipe && .venv/bin/python main.py --config config_project.yaml >> cron.log 2>&1
```
Windows: Task Scheduler -> weekly -> program `.venv\Scripts\python.exe`, arguments `main.py --config config_project.yaml`, start in the project folder.

## 7. Good to know
* **Query syntax differs per database.** The default `query` suits OpenAlex. Add `pubmed:`, `arxiv:`, `semantic_scholar:` overrides for exact
  semantics; otherwise a rough translation is used (and a warning is logged if the query has OR/NOT).
* **Only open-access PDFs.** Paywalled papers end as `no_pdf`/`failed` and fall back to abstract-only extraction (`input_basis` column).
  If you legitimately have a PDF (e.g. via your library), save it as `pdfs/<doi with / replaced by _>.pdf` and it will be picked up.
* **Scanned PDFs** are flagged `needs_ocr` (no OCR built in). Run `ocrmypdf` on them, then `--stages pdf,extract`.
* **Verify the output.** LLM extraction is a screening aid: spot-check sample sizes and statistics against the paper, especially for
  papers with multiple experiments. Prompts instruct the model to return null rather than guess.
* **Cost.** Each paper is one LLM call of up to `max_input_chars` (~15k tokens at the default). Start with `--limit`.
* **Local LLMs:** set `provider: openai`, `base_url: http://localhost:11434/v1`, `model: <your model>` (Ollama/LM Studio). Small models are
  less reliable at JSON and numbers.
